      "id": 1,
      "text": "The town was renamed Littlerock after a prominent mounting stone on Thomas Rutledge’s homestead.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littlerock%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Rutledge opened a post office at his homestead in 1879, becoming the first postmaster until 1883.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/littlerock_washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Just outside town lies the Mima Mounds Natural Area Preserve, a National Natural Landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littlerock%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1909 Littlerock boasted two shingle mills employing 330 workers and producing 20,000 feet of lumber daily.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
